1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,068
Lesson 0.4.

2
00:00:02,448 --> 00:00:04,130
Linear algebra refresher.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:09,472
Almost every thermodynamic algorithm
in this course ends up in a matrix.

4
00:00:09,852 --> 00:00:12,623
The thermodynamic linear solver relaxes a

5
00:00:12,623 --> 00:00:15,596
system whose dynamics
are set by a matrix A.

6
00:00:15,976 --> 00:00:18,589
Its speed is limited by
the condition number.

7
00:00:18,969 --> 00:00:22,228
Thermodynamic natural
gradient uses the Woodbury

8
00:00:22,228 --> 00:00:25,962
identity, and the K-FAC method
uses Kronecker products.

9
00:00:26,342 --> 00:00:30,188
In this lesson we review those
tools with a geometric eye.

10
00:00:31,317 --> 00:00:34,568
A two-by-two matrix is a
transformation of the plane.

11
00:00:34,948 --> 00:00:38,826
Look at what it does to the unit
circle: it turns it into an ellipse.

12
00:00:39,206 --> 00:00:42,213
If the matrix is symmetric,
there are two perpendicular

13
00:00:42,213 --> 00:00:44,783
directions that only
stretch, without rotating.

14
00:00:45,163 --> 00:00:48,656
If it is also positive
definite, both stretches are

15
00:00:48,656 --> 00:00:51,738
positive: the matrix
never flips a direction.

16
00:00:52,118 --> 00:00:55,900
Another way to see it:
the function ½ xᵀ A x is a

17
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:59,065
bowl with a single minimum at the origin.

18
00:01:00,183 --> 00:01:06,109
Eigenvectors are the directions the
matrix does not rotate: A v = λ v.

19
00:01:06,489 --> 00:01:11,268
A symmetric matrix has orthogonal
eigenvectors and real eigenvalues.

20
00:01:11,648 --> 00:01:15,844
That is why it decomposes as
A = Q Λ Qᵀ: first we rotate to

21
00:01:15,844 --> 00:01:20,395
align the eigen-axes with the
coordinate axes, then stretch each

22
00:01:20,395 --> 00:01:24,234
axis by its eigenvalue, and
finally undo the rotation.

23
00:01:24,614 --> 00:01:27,847
The animation applies those
three steps one by one,

24
00:01:27,847 --> 00:01:30,573
and the result matches
applying A directly.

25
00:01:31,683 --> 00:01:38,134
The condition number, κ = λ_max / λ_min,
measures how elongated the bowl is.

26
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:42,076
With κ close to one, the level
curves are almost circles

27
00:01:42,076 --> 00:01:45,256
and gradient descent goes
straight to the minimum.

28
00:01:45,636 --> 00:01:49,358
With large κ, the valley
is narrow: gradient descent

29
00:01:49,358 --> 00:01:53,223
bounces from wall to wall
and crawls along the bottom.

30
00:01:53,603 --> 00:01:55,554
The two trajectories you see are real

31
00:01:55,554 --> 00:01:57,821
computations with the
same number of steps.

32
00:01:58,201 --> 00:02:02,323
In thermodynamic algorithms, κ
appears as the ratio between the

33
00:02:02,323 --> 00:02:06,118
slowest and the fastest time
scale of the physical system.

34
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:11,175
Consider the dynamics ẋ = −A x.

35
00:02:11,555 --> 00:02:17,687
Its solution is x(t) = e^(−At)
x₀, the exponential of a matrix.

36
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:23,897
In the eigenbasis it is simple:
each component decays as e^(−λᵢ t).

37
00:02:24,277 --> 00:02:27,464
The component with a large
eigenvalue vanishes fast;

38
00:02:27,464 --> 00:02:30,528
the one with a small
eigenvalue takes a long time.

39
00:02:30,908 --> 00:02:33,857
That is why all trajectories
first fall onto the

40
00:02:33,857 --> 00:02:36,499
slow axis and then
creep toward the origin.

41
00:02:36,879 --> 00:02:40,553
The total relaxation time is
set by the smallest eigenvalue,

42
00:02:40,553 --> 00:02:43,675
and the integration step
is limited by the largest.

43
00:02:44,055 --> 00:02:45,870
Their ratio is κ again.

44
00:02:46,983 --> 00:02:51,078
The Kronecker product B ⊗
C builds a big matrix by

45
00:02:51,078 --> 00:02:54,844
placing a copy of C
scaled by each entry of B.

46
00:02:55,224 --> 00:02:59,696
Two n-by-n matrices produce
one that is n² by n².

47
00:03:00,076 --> 00:03:05,541
The valuable part is that you never
need to form it: (B ⊗ C) vec(X) = vec(C

48
00:03:05,541 --> 00:03:10,204
X Bᵀ), and the inverse is the
Kronecker product of the inverses.

49
00:03:10,584 --> 00:03:15,056
Inverting two small matrices costs
far less than inverting one big one.

50
00:03:15,436 --> 00:03:18,546
This is the idea behind the
K-FAC method of unit eight.

51
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:22,372
The Woodbury identity says how the inverse

52
00:03:22,372 --> 00:03:25,094
changes when we add a low-rank correction.

53
00:03:25,474 --> 00:03:30,376
If we know the inverse of A, the
inverse of A plus U C V only requires

54
00:03:30,376 --> 00:03:35,349
inverting a small k-by-k matrix,
where k is the rank of the correction.

55
00:03:35,729 --> 00:03:38,890
The colored blocks show
the sizes: instead of

56
00:03:38,890 --> 00:03:42,754
inverting something d by d,
we invert something k by k.

57
00:03:43,134 --> 00:03:48,307
In machine learning, the curvature matrix
is often a multiple of the identity plus a

58
00:03:48,307 --> 00:03:52,679
term whose rank equals the batch
size, and Woodbury makes it tractable.

59
00:03:53,783 --> 00:03:56,754
Take A with rows three, one and one, two.

60
00:03:57,134 --> 00:03:58,008
It is symmetric.

61
00:03:58,388 --> 00:04:06,126
Its eigenvalues are five plus or minus
root five, over two: about 3.62 and 1.38.

62
00:04:06,506 --> 00:04:09,500
Both are positive, so
it is positive definite.

63
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:13,848
Its condition number is about 2.62.

64
00:04:14,228 --> 00:04:17,592
The determinant is the
product of the eigenvalues,

65
00:04:17,592 --> 00:04:20,551
five, and the trace is
their sum, also five.

66
00:04:21,683 --> 00:04:24,607
In the course, the condition
number will determine

67
00:04:24,607 --> 00:04:27,354
how long the thermodynamic
linear solver takes.

68
00:04:27,734 --> 00:04:29,909
The matrix exponential describes the

69
00:04:29,909 --> 00:04:32,628
relaxation of the
Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process.

70
00:04:33,008 --> 00:04:37,512
Kronecker products are the heart of
K-FAC and of its thermodynamic version.

71
00:04:37,892 --> 00:04:43,025
And Woodbury lets us invert low-rank
curvature matrices in the natural gradient.

72
00:04:44,150 --> 00:04:47,613
These ideas connect with what
we have seen and what is coming.

73
00:04:47,993 --> 00:04:52,330
The covariance of a Gaussian is a
symmetric positive-definite matrix.

74
00:04:52,710 --> 00:04:56,613
The spectral gap of a Markov
chain is also an eigenvalue.

75
00:04:56,993 --> 00:05:01,621
The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process is the
matrix exponential with noise, and the

76
00:05:01,621 --> 00:05:06,066
stability of its numerical integration
depends on the largest eigenvalue.

77
00:05:07,183 --> 00:05:08,758
Beware of four mistakes.

78
00:05:09,138 --> 00:05:12,955
Symmetric does not mean
positive definite: the matrix

79
00:05:12,955 --> 00:05:16,196
one, two, two, one has
a negative eigenvalue.

80
00:05:16,576 --> 00:05:19,895
All entries being positive
guarantees nothing either.

81
00:05:20,275 --> 00:05:22,941
The exponential of a sum
is not the product of

82
00:05:22,941 --> 00:05:25,490
exponentials if the
matrices do not commute.

83
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:28,190
And never form the Kronecker product

84
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:31,475
explicitly: use the identity
with the vec operator.

85
00:05:32,583 --> 00:05:36,765
In summary: a symmetric
positive-definite matrix

86
00:05:36,765 --> 00:05:41,557
stretches space along orthogonal
axes, with A = Q Λ Qᵀ.

87
00:05:41,937 --> 00:05:44,238
The condition number measures the ratio

88
00:05:44,238 --> 00:05:46,716
between the slowest and the fastest scale.

89
00:05:47,096 --> 00:05:50,754
The matrix exponential
solves linear relaxation, and

90
00:05:50,754 --> 00:05:55,044
Kronecker and Woodbury turn
giant inversions into small ones.

91
00:05:56,150 --> 00:05:59,415
Check what you learned:
prove that a symmetric

92
00:05:59,415 --> 00:06:03,390
positive-definite matrix has
real, positive eigenvalues.

93
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:08,108
Write the inverse of a Kronecker
product and its action on vec of X.

94
00:06:08,488 --> 00:06:12,589
Solve the relaxation with a diagonal
matrix with values one and one hundred.

95
00:06:12,969 --> 00:06:18,043
And use Woodbury to invert lambda times
the identity plus a rank-one correction.
